## 2. Coding Standards & Architectural Rules

All contributions and modules in this repository adhere to the following rules:

1. **Google Python Style Guide**: All Python source and test files follow the
   [Google Python Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html),
   including 2-space indentation, `CapWords` class names, `lower_with_under`
   function and variable names, module-level docstrings, `Args:` / `Returns:` /
   `Raises:` docstring blocks, and PEP 484 type annotations.
2. **Protocol Buffers Style Guide**: All `.proto` definitions follow the
   [Google Protobuf Style Guide](https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/style/),
   using `CamelCase` message and enum names, `lower_snake_case` field names, and
   `CAPITALS_WITH_UNDERSCORES` enum values prefixed with the enum type name.
   All input submessages are nested inside `SimulationConfig`, and all output
   submessages are nested inside `SimulationOutput`.
3. **Abseil Python Application Structure**: Executables use `absl.app.run()`,
   `absl.flags` for command-line flag parsing, and `absl.logging` for structured
   diagnostic output. Unit tests use `absl.testing.absltest`.
4. **Explicit Enum Value Assignment**: Every `enum` in both `.proto` files and
   Python `enum.IntEnum` classes must assign integer values manually (never
   using `enum.auto()`), starting with `UNSPECIFIED = 0`.
5. **Single-Run Scope**: The core simulator executes a single deterministic
   configuration per invocation. Parametric sweeps belong in external scripts.
6. **Immutable Inputs & Cryptographic Provenance**: Input `.textproto` files are
   never modified by the simulator. Upon loading, the simulator computes the
   raw-byte `md5sum` of the input file and embeds both the hexadecimal MD5
   digest and a full `input_config_snapshot` inside the timestamped
   `SimulationOutput` `.out.textproto` file.
7. **Ergonomic Single-Patch Inspection**: While vectorized solver kernels use
   contiguous `numpy.ndarray` Structure-of-Arrays (SoA) fields on `SurfaceMesh`,
   inspection and debugging code should use `SurfaceMesh.get_patch()`,
   `SurfaceMesh.get_patches_by_type()`, and `SurfaceMesh.find_patch_at_coord()`
   to obtain self-contained `PatchView` objects.

